stability analysis, and frequency response methods for both analyzing existing systems and designing compensators to meet performance specifications. The course then transitions to discrete-time and state-space representations of linear time-invariant systems, reviewing the linear algebra foundations needed to characterize controllability, observability, and stabilizability, and culminating in state feedback controller design via pole placement. Together, these topics equip students with both the classical frequency-domain toolkit and the modern state-space framework essential for analyzing and designing control systems across engineering applications. The course will be accompanied with an experimental controls laboratory."}]},{"id":"SE 400","title":"Engineering Law","creditHours":"3 OR 4 hours.","sections":[{"crn":"66937","code":"B","type":"Lecture-Discussion","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Lecture-Discussion","days":["R"],"start":960,"end":1100,"location":"160 English Building","instructors":["Barich, J"]}],"restrictions":["Restricted to students with Junior or Senior class standing."],"note":""},{"crn":"68225","code":"C","type":"Lecture-Discussion","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Lecture-Discussion","days":["R"],"start":960,"end":1100,"location":"160 English Building","instructors":["Barich, J"]}],"restrictions":["Restricted to Graduate - Urbana-Champaign."],"note":""}]},{"id":"SE 411","title":"Reliability Engineering","creditHours":"3 OR 4 hours.","sections":[{"crn":"66939","code":"G","type":"Lecture","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Lecture","days":["T","R"],"start":840,"end":920,"location":"432 Armory","instructors":["Wang, P"]}],"restrictions":["Restricted to Graduate - Urbana-Champaign."],"note":""},{"crn":"66938","code":"U","type":"Lecture","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Lecture","days":["T","R"],"start":840,"end":920,"location":"432 Armory","instructors":["Wang, P"]}],"restrictions":["Restricted to Graduate - Urbana-Champaign or U